3. **Replication**: Although not directly part of the flow from DNA to RNA to proteins, replication is the process by which DNA is copied before cell division, which is essential for the transmission of genetic information from one generation to the next. It is catalyzed by the enzyme DNA polymerase.

The central dogma was named by Francis Crick, who first proposed it in 1958. Crick was a key figure in the discovery of the structure of DNA and made significant contributions to our understanding of genetics and molecular biology.

